  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1202 AM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nThursday Night through Tuesday:\n\nHigh pressure should move to the east of the area Thursday \nnight. Lows may drop into the lower to middle 40s Thursday \nnight, as the high brings light winds and clear skies. \n\nWarm air advection and 850 mb frontogenesis response should \ndevelop over Iowa Thursday night, then spread east southeast \nacross portions of the area on Friday. A surface warm front \nshould also move northeast through the area on Friday. These \nfeatures may combine with some moisture in the low levels to \nbring 20 to 30 percent chances for showers on Friday. Ensemble \nmembers are mixed on these precipitation chances, with roughly \nhalf of the members at best showing any measurable rainfall. \nForecast soundings do not show enough instability for a thunder \nmention at this time.\n\nThe warm front should push north of the area Friday night into \nSaturday, with increasing south to southwest winds developing as\nthe pressure gradient tightens across the region. Ensembles are\nin good agreement with showing warm temperatures across the \narea Saturday into Sunday. Highs into the upper 70s to perhaps \n80 degrees or higher are forecast each day. \n\nThe remnants of the tropical cyclone that moves northeast into \nthe Ohio Valley Saturday night into Sunday night may or may not \nhave much influence on our area. It will depend on its track and\nhow expansive any rain shield with it becomes. For now, kept \nmost of the area dry until more confidence is gained with where \nthat system may track. It may end up being just more clouds on \nSunday, which may hold down temperatures a bit. \n\nModels and ensembles generally are showing a slow-moving cold \nfront sagging southward across the region Monday into Tuesday of\nnext week, which may bring the next decent chances (20 to 40 \npercent range) for rain to the area. Ensemble members are \nshowing most members with at least measurable precipitation at \nsome point in this period. Temperatures should cool down to more\nseasonable values per ensemble trends.\n\nWood",
